    "id": 5,
    "category": "OMM: Principles of Cranial Diagnosis",
    "questionText": "A physician motion tests a patient's cranium through several flexion phases and finds that the head moves easily into left sidebending-rotation but meets a firm, unyielding barrier when right sidebending-rotation is intended. The rest of the patterns test symmetrically. The physician now needs to record the finding in the chart before choosing a treatment approach. How should this be documented, and why?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Right sidebending-rotation, because the restricted side identifies the lesion", "explanation": "Incorrect. Naming for the restricted side reverses the osteopathic convention and would make an indirect treatment carry the pattern into its barrier instead of its freedom. The restriction is real information, but it is not the name. This would be the correct answer if osteopathic somatic dysfunctions were named for their barriers, which they are not." },
      { "text": "Bilateral sidebending-rotation, because both directions were tested in the same cycle", "explanation": "Incorrect. Testing both directions is simply the method of comparison; it does not create a bilateral diagnosis. The lecture instructs the operator to test one direction, allow a full flexion and extension cycle, then test the opposite direction and compare. This would be the correct answer if both directions moved equally and freely, which would instead indicate no dysfunction at all." },
      { "text": "Sphenobasilar compression, because a firm barrier in any direction defines compression", "explanation": "Incorrect. Sphenobasilar compression has no axes of motion at all and feels globally rock-hard and heavy, described as a bowling-ball head, rather than restricted in one direction with free motion in the other. A unidirectional barrier does not meet that description. This would be the correct answer if the head had essentially no discernible motion in any direction tested." },
      { "text": "Left sidebending-rotation, because dysfunctions are named for the freedom", "explanation": "Correct. Motion testing compares the two directions of a pattern, and the direction with more motion is the freedom. Cranial somatic dysfunctions, like somatic dysfunctions everywhere else in osteopathic diagnosis, are named for the freedom, so the finding is a left sidebending-rotation. Sidebending-rotations are additionally named for the side of fullness or convexity, which is the side whose hand moves caudally with the fingers spreading. Naming for the freedom also makes indirect treatment intuitive: it carries the pattern further into the direction named in the diagnosis." },
      { "text": "No dysfunction, because sidebending-rotation is a physiologic pattern of the mechanism", "explanation": "Incorrect. Physiologic describes the mechanics of the pattern, meaning the occiput and sphenoid move in opposite directions around their axes, not whether a dysfunction exists. A physiologic pattern can absolutely be held asymmetrically and require treatment. This would be the correct answer if the head moved equally well into both directions of every pattern tested." }
    ],
