    "id": 9,
    "category": "OMM: V-Spread and Directing the Tide",
    "questionText": "A technique is described to a student as follows: two fingers of one hand are placed on either side of a restricted suture and exert gentle spread and traction, while one or two fingers of the other hand are placed at the point of greatest distance from that suture on the opposite side of the skull and initiate a fluid fluctuation toward it. The student is asked to classify the technique. Which two technique classifications does it combine?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Muscle energy and myofascial release, applied to the sutural membranes", "explanation": "Incorrect. Muscle energy requires an active patient contraction against a physician counterforce, and neither hand in this description is resisting anything the patient does. No active patient effort appears anywhere in the technique. This would be the correct answer if the patient were asked to contract against resistance in a controlled direction and the physician then engaged a new barrier." },
      { "text": "Direct balanced membranous tension and indirect balanced membranous tension", "explanation": "Incorrect. Both forms of balanced membranous tension work with a whole strain pattern carried to a point of balanced tension, not with two fingers straddling a single suture while a distant hand launches a fluid wave. The described technique also has no barrier-versus-freedom decision. This would be the correct answer if the operator were carrying a diagnosed torsion or sidebending-rotation toward either its barrier or its freedom." },
      { "text": "Articulatory technique and high velocity low amplitude thrust, scaled down", "explanation": "Incorrect. Neither repetitive articulation through a range nor a short rapid thrust appears in this description, and both would represent far more force than the lightest-possible-force principle this technique demands. The intent here is a fluid effect, not an articular one. This would be the correct answer if the suture were being repetitively sprung through its range or given a corrective impulse." },
      { "text": "Exaggeration and counterstrain, held for ninety seconds at the point of ease", "explanation": "Incorrect. Counterstrain requires a tender point and a position of ease held for a timed interval, neither of which is present, and exaggeration is explicitly set aside as a classification not covered in this lab. The endpoint here is palpated tissue change, not a clock. This would be the correct answer if a monitored tender point were held in a shortened position for approximately ninety seconds." },
      { "text": "Disengagement and directing the tide", "explanation": "Correct. The lecture states plainly that V-spread is a combination of disengagement and directing the tide. The spreading fingers disengage the suture, and the contralateral hand directs a fluid fluctuation toward it using intention rather than pressure. That fluctuation is palpated between the V-spread fingers, may bounce back to the initiating hand, and is received and redirected for several cycles until a release or apparent softening of the tissues occurs." }
    ],
