    "options": [
      { "text": "Pressure on the frontal risks driving the frontal bone into an internally rotated position", "explanation": "Incorrect. Although heavy-handed contacts anywhere on the cranium can distort bony position, this is not the reason the lecture gives for using intention. The stated rationale is about how little muscular effort intention requires of the operator. This would be the correct answer if the instruction concerned avoiding iatrogenic positional change of a paired lateral bone under the contact." },
      { "text": "Intention contracts the fewest muscle fibers and therefore applies the slightest force", "explanation": "Correct. The lecture states that the physician uses intention to initiate the wave because this method contracts the fewest muscle fibers and so applies the slightest force. The technique depends on the fluctuation of cerebrospinal fluid to release the restriction rather than on mechanical displacement of fluid across the skull. The same principle governs motion testing, where the operator is told to think about performing the motion rather than actually performing it. Using the lightest possible force also protects against inducing a new strain pattern." },
      { "text": "Intention avoids stimulating the cutaneous mechanoreceptors that mask the fluid wave", "explanation": "Incorrect. Sensory masking is a plausible-sounding idea but is not the rationale given, and the lecture frames the issue entirely in terms of the operator's own muscular effort. Palpatory sensitivity is addressed separately, through relaxing your own body. This would be the correct answer if the lecture had attributed the failure of heavy contacts to receptor adaptation in the patient's scalp." },
      { "text": "Physical pressure would compress the superior sagittal sinus and impede venous drainage", "explanation": "Incorrect. No such mechanism is described, and the directing hand for an occipitomastoid V-spread rests on the frontal rather than over the midline sinus. Venous drainage concerns in this lecture attach to the jugular foramen and the internal jugular vein. This would be the correct answer if the question concerned the consequences of a restriction near the confluence of sinuses." },
      { "text": "Cerebrospinal fluid is incompressible, so no amount of applied pressure can move it at all", "explanation": "Incorrect. The lecture actually relies on the constancy of intracranial volume elsewhere, in the CV4 mechanism, where added compression is transmitted to the ventricular system. The point about intention is about operator force, not about fluid physics. This would be the correct answer if the question asked why compression at the occiput raises pressure within the ventricular system." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: intention, not pressure - it contracts the fewest muscle fibers and applies the slightest force, and the goal is a fluid fluctuation rather than a physical push.",
    "pdfPage": 22,
    "pdfQuote": "contracts the fewest muscle fibers and so applies the slightest force."
