    "id": 13,
    "category": "OMM: CV4",
    "questionText": "A physician is seated at the head of the table with both forearms resting on it, has interlaced her fingers, and is cradling the patient's occipital squama in preparation for a compression of the fourth ventricle. Before applying any medial force she checks the position of both thenar eminences against the bony landmarks. Where must they be sitting?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Posteromedial to the occipitomastoid sutures, inferior to the inion", "explanation": "Correct. The thenar eminences are placed posteromedial to the occipitomastoid sutures - inside them - and the lecturer describes the position as inferior to the inion and medial to both occipitomastoid sutures. This puts the compressive force on the occipital squama itself rather than on the temporal bones. From there the physician encourages extension with medial springing and then resists flexion, holding the occiput in extension with bilateral medial force until a still point is reached. Correct placement is the safety step of the technique." },
      { "text": "Directly over the mastoid processes of the temporal bones on each side", "explanation": "Incorrect. This is the specific error the source warns against, because medial compression delivered at the mastoids bilaterally externally rotates the temporal bones and may cause extreme untoward reactions. The mastoid is outside the occipitomastoid suture, not inside it. This would be the correct answer if the intent were to mobilize the temporal bones themselves, which is emphatically not the intent of CV4." },
      { "text": "Over the asterion bilaterally, where the vault contact has most amplitude", "explanation": "Incorrect. The asterion is a landmark used in vault contact discussions and lies well lateral and superior to the target area, so contact there would not deliver medial compression to the occipital squama. Landmark familiarity does not make a landmark a treatment contact. This would be the correct answer if the technique required a parietal-level contact at a former fontanelle site." },
      { "text": "Lateral to the occipitomastoid sutures, spanning the temporal squama", "explanation": "Incorrect. Lateral to the sutures means on the temporal side, which carries the same hazard as contact on the mastoid processes and moves the force off the occiput entirely. The instruction is unambiguously medial. This would be the correct answer if the technique aimed to induce bilateral external rotation of the temporals, which is the adverse event to avoid." },
      { "text": "On the superior nuchal line, with the inion resting between the two hands", "explanation": "Incorrect. This describes the cradling position used in the unilateral temporal hold, where the inion rests in the palm and the middle finger and palm lie along the superior nuchal line. It is a diagnostic contact for the temporal bone, not the CV4 contact. This would be the correct answer if the physician were setting up to sense motion at a single temporal bone." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: CV4 thenar eminences go inferior to the inion and posteromedial to the OM sutures - inside them, never on the mastoids.",
    "pdfPage": 24,
    "pdfQuote": "places the thenar eminences posteromedial to the patient's"
