    "id": 16,
    "category": "OMM: CV4",
    "questionText": "A 52-year-old woman presents with a 6-month history of daily bilateral pressure-type headaches with no aura, photophobia or neurologic deficit. Vitals are temperature 36.8 C, blood pressure 122/76 mmHg, pulse 70/min, respirations 14/min. Cranial exam shows a low-amplitude cranial rhythmic impulse and cervical paraspinal hypertonicity. The physician performs a CV4 with the thenar eminences correctly placed medial to the occipitomastoid sutures. After roughly 90 seconds she notes the amplitude of the impulse falling away, a bead of sweat appears on the patient's brow, and the patient's breathing pattern changes. What is the appropriate next action?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Increase the medial compressive force to deepen the effect before releasing", "explanation": "Incorrect. The findings described are the endpoint of the technique, not a signal to escalate, and adding force at this stage is both unnecessary and contrary to the lightest-force principle throughout this lecture. The technique works by holding, not by pressing harder. This would be the correct answer if no change in amplitude or tissue quality had occurred after a prolonged hold, and even then the corrective step would be to reassess rather than to add force." },
      { "text": "Immediately lift both hands so the mechanism is not held past its physiologic limit", "explanation": "Incorrect. Abrupt withdrawal is discouraged throughout this lecture; even after a balanced membranous tension release the operator is told to wait several cycles of inhalation and exhalation rather than take the hands off right away. The release here should be slow and should follow the resumption of the impulse. This would be the correct answer if a genuine adverse reaction had developed, such as the untoward response produced by mastoid contact." },
      { "text": "Reposition the thenar eminences onto the mastoid processes to broaden the contact", "explanation": "Incorrect. Mastoid contact is the specific hazard the technique description warns against, because medial compression there bilaterally externally rotates the temporal bones and may cause extreme untoward reactions. Broadening the contact in that direction converts a safe technique into an unsafe one. This would be the correct answer if the intent were to deliberately induce bilateral external rotation of the temporal bones, which is never the intent of this technique." },
      { "text": "Convert to a V-spread of the occipitomastoid sutures while the tissues remain soft", "explanation": "Incorrect. Switching techniques mid-hold abandons a CV4 at the exact moment it is achieving its endpoint, and the V-spread requires its own screening step and its own hand placement. Sutural work is a reasonable part of the same visit, just not an interruption of this one. This would be the correct answer if the CV4 had already been completed and one occipitomastoid suture had screened as heavy and compressed." },
      { "text": "Recognize a still point, then release slowly as the CRI resumes and retest it", "explanation": "Correct. Decreasing amplitude of the cranial rhythmic impulse, a still point, and signs of release such as softening and warmth, a bead of sweat on the brow, or a change in respiration are the described endpoints of a CV4. Once they appear, the force is no longer maintained; as the impulse resumes, the physician slowly releases and allows the mechanism its newfound excursion. The rate and amplitude of the impulse are then retested to assess the effect of the technique. Objective retesting matters because subjective improvement may lag behind the palpable change." }
    ],
