    "id": 27,
    "category": "OMM: Balanced Membranous Tension",
    "questionText": "A student watching a treatment asks how a light contact on the vault of the skull can possibly influence a strain that the physician says is located deep at the cranial base, given that no force appears to be transmitted through bone. The physician answers by describing the tissue that connects the two regions and explains that it is both the reason a strain is held and the means by which it can be diagnosed and released. Which explanation is most consistent with this lecture?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "The dural membranes guide and limit cranial motion and transmit tension throughout", "explanation": "Correct. Sutherland named the principle balanced ligamentous tension in the body, and in the head the equivalent tissue is membrane. The dura is called the reciprocal tension membrane because the vertically placed falx and the roughly horizontal, tented tentorium hold tension against one another. Because the falx cerebri runs from the crista galli of the ethmoid to the confluence of sinuses and the tentorium runs from there along the petrous ridge to the clinoid processes of the sphenoid, tension anywhere in the system is felt everywhere. That is why a strain pattern can be motion tested and treated through the dura from any contact." },
      { "text": "The cranial bones are rigidly fused in adults, so force is conducted directly through bone", "explanation": "Incorrect. Rigid fusion is the premise this entire field rejects, and the lecture emphasizes 143 craniofacial articulations available for motion. If the bones were fused there would be no strain patterns to diagnose. This would be the correct answer if the model of the adult cranium in this lecture were a single immobile unit." },
      { "text": "Cerebrospinal fluid pressure equalizes instantly, so any contact changes intracranial pressure", "explanation": "Incorrect. Constant intracranial volume is invoked in this lecture to explain the CV4 mechanism, not to explain how a vault contact reaches a strain at the base. Fluid physics and membranous tension are separate arguments here. This would be the correct answer if the question asked why occipital compression is transmitted to the ventricular system." },
      { "text": "The scalp fascia is continuous with the falx and carries the load from the vault to the base", "explanation": "Incorrect. The connecting tissue described is intracranial dura, not the scalp fascia, and no continuity of that kind is claimed. Attributing the effect to the scalp misses the reciprocal tension membrane entirely. This would be the correct answer if the technique in question were a superficial myofascial release of the scalp." },
      { "text": "Only the temporal bone can transmit tension to the base, through the sphenosquamous pivot", "explanation": "Incorrect. The sphenosquamous pivot is a real articulation of the temporal with the sphenoid and is listed among the temporal's connections, but it is not the sole route by which tension reaches the base. The membranes connect the whole system. This would be the correct answer if the question asked which articulation links the temporal to the sphenoid." }
    ],
